WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.784 --> 00:00:03.270
[Narrator] "Midsommar" is a horror movie

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:07.000
about a Swedish festival
gone very, very wrong.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.840
The film was written and
directed by Ari Aster,

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.620
the man behind 2018's "Hereditary."

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:16.890
This follow-up is equally
disturbing and just as baffling.

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:19.330
Here are all of the symbols
and hidden references

00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:23.210
in the movie, and how they
relate to its shocking ending.

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:25.023
Warning: Spoilers ahead.

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:28.170
First of all, while "Midsommar"

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:30.930
quickly turns into a cultish nightmare,

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:34.090
the movie is ultimately about
the end of a relationship

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:37.400
between the two main
characters, Dani and Christian.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:40.280
Ari Aster was recovering
from a breakup of his own

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:42.210
while he was developing the film,

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:44.081
and that experience influenced

00:00:44.081 --> 00:00:45.681
his writing of the characters,

00:00:45.681 --> 00:00:47.970
in particular Dani.

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:50.290
He was also inspired by romantic films,

00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:53.400
like 1973's "Scenes From a Marriage"

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.167
and 1981's "Modern Romance."

00:00:56.167 --> 00:00:58.337
Robert: I don't think that
we should go out anymore.

00:00:58.337 --> 00:01:00.510
[Narrator] As for his horror influences,

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:02.700
there's a clear reference to "The Shining"

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:04.923
in this overhead shot of the journey.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:09.290
Early in the movie, the
paintings on the walls

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:12.520
of the apartments hint
toward the ending of the film

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:16.100
and the very messy breakup we witness.

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:18.210
In Christian's apartment,
we see a painting

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:20.750
by Brooklyn-based artist Mu Pan.

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:24.180
The work is part of a series
called "Dinoassholes,"

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:25.970
a title that seems very appropriate

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:27.240
for Christian's character,

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:29.540
given his callous treatment of Dani.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:33.300
Also visible is this work by
Swedish artist John Bauer.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:37.030
It depicts a little girl in
a crown facing a large bear.

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:39.630
This painting directly
alludes to the movie's ending,

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:42.470
when Christian is placed
inside a bear carcass

00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:44.030
and set on fire.

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:47.720
Dani watches him burn while
wearing a crown of flowers.

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:49.220
Later, in Siv's house,

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:51.310
Christian also sees an image on the wall

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:53.620
of a bear being burned alive,

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:56.454
further foreshadowing his untimely death.

00:01:56.454 --> 00:01:58.906
Simon: So we're just going
to ignore the bear then?

00:01:58.906 --> 00:01:59.744
Ingemar: It's a bear.

00:01:59.744 --> 00:02:00.577
[Narrator] Aster compared

00:02:00.577 --> 00:02:02.810
the sacrificial burning of the temple

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:05.745
to the burning of a box
of an ex's personal items

00:02:05.745 --> 00:02:08.220
as a sort of catharsis post-breakup,

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:10.890
only, of course, far more extreme.

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:12.720
The sacrificial burning at the end

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:15.400
also calls back the ending
of "The Wicker Man,"

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.330
in which the outsider main character

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:20.350
is placed in a wooden
statue and set afire.

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:22.350
Aster and his team spent months

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:25.766
researching Scandinavian
and Germanic folklore

00:02:25.766 --> 00:02:27.730
and scattered Easter
eggs throughout the film,

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:29.480
which also hint at the culmination

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:31.738
of Dani and Christian's relationship,

00:02:31.738 --> 00:02:33.221
major plot points,

00:02:33.221 --> 00:02:35.120
and other characters' deaths.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:38.070
One of the most important
set pieces, the bunkhouse,

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:40.670
exemplifies this attention to detail.

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:43.610
The inner walls are
covered in elaborate murals

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:48.320
painted by artist Ragnar Persson
and based on medieval art.

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:49.603
If you look closely,

00:02:49.603 --> 00:02:52.380
these images actually
reveal much of the plot.

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:55.110
Basically, the bunkhouse
fulfills the same function

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:57.720
as the dollhouse did in "Hereditary,"

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:01.680
illustrating much of the story
before it actually happens.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:03.640
For example, above one of the beds

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:06.590
we see an illustration
of two people having sex,

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:08.560
surrounded by onlookers.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:10.860
This clearly refers to the mating ritual

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:14.730
Christian is later lured
into to impregnate Maja.

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:16.305
This scene is also hinted at

00:03:16.305 --> 00:03:18.277
when the boys are eating
earlier in the film

00:03:18.277 --> 00:03:20.243
and discussing all of the Swedish girls

00:03:20.243 --> 00:03:23.060
they plan on impregnating during the trip.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:24.840
Another illustration shows people

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:27.130
cutting their hands as a sacrifice.

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:29.770
Soon after, we see the
old couple cut their palms

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:33.440
atop the cliff, right before
they jump to their deaths.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:35.750
Also covering the walls are many runes,

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:37.920
an ancient language that features heavily

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:39.460
throughout the film.

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:41.830
At one point, the characters
refer to the runes

00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:43.321
as Elder Futhark

00:03:43.321 --> 00:03:44.704
and Younger Futhark,

00:03:44.704 --> 00:03:47.940
two evolutions of the
same system of language.

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:50.608
Looking at the meanings
of some of these symbols,

00:03:50.608 --> 00:03:53.370
we can try to piece together
why Aster chose them

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:55.400
in certain parts of the movie.

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:57.600
He assigned each character a rune,

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:00.085
which we can see clearly on their robes.

00:04:00.085 --> 00:04:02.250
Dani has an R rune on her dress,

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:04.780
which symbolizes a ride or a journey.

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:07.663
This likely refers to her
own journey of finally

00:04:07.663 --> 00:04:10.820
coming to terms with the
truth about her relationship.

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:13.095
The hourglass-shaped rune next it

00:04:13.095 --> 00:04:15.445
symbolizes day or an awakening.

00:04:15.445 --> 00:04:18.900
This likely refers to Dani's
breakthrough about Christian,

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:20.600
a moment of clarity for her.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:24.369
Dani's character also
experiences a slow reawakening

00:04:24.369 --> 00:04:25.420
throughout the festival.

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:27.040
The first sign of her rebirth

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:28.800
is when she wakes up from her nap

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:32.613
and suddenly her skin appears
golden and almost glittery.

00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:36.140
Meanwhile, Christian's
robe has an up-arrow rune,

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:38.670
which could reference
either the male symbol

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:41.270
or a willingness to self-sacrifice,

00:04:41.270 --> 00:04:44.290
ironically, a quality he does not have.

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:46.420
The first bloody sacrifice we witness

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:48.960
is that of two senior
members of the community

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:51.010
who throw themselves off a cliff.

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:53.944
This tradition is called ättestupa.

00:04:53.944 --> 00:04:56.306
It's based off of Nordic
legends in which people

00:04:56.306 --> 00:04:58.013
would commit ritual suicide

00:04:58.013 --> 00:05:01.080
when they grew too old to care
for themselves any longer.

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:03.390
When the man and woman
rub their bloody hands

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:04.665
on the rune stones,

00:05:04.665 --> 00:05:06.007
the two symbols that appear

00:05:06.007 --> 00:05:07.814
are the R from Dani's robe

00:05:07.814 --> 00:05:10.200
and the up arrow from Christian's robe.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:13.420
The shape of the dining
table is also significant.

00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:15.590
This rune symbolizes tradition,

00:05:15.590 --> 00:05:18.510
the passing down of rituals and rites.

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:21.930
If you look closely at the
sunlike entrance to the village,

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:25.202
you'll see the same
shape, but upside-down.

00:05:25.202 --> 00:05:28.340
The X inside the sacred
temple can mean gift,

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:30.815
which makes sense, as
the community is planning

00:05:30.815 --> 00:05:33.393
on gifting nine souls to the gods.

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:35.785
Beyond the bunkhouse murals,

00:05:35.785 --> 00:05:37.490
there's a tapestry in the open field

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:40.050
that also foretells parts of the plot.

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:41.960
It depicts a bizarre love story

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:45.050
in which a girl puts her
menstrual blood and pubic hair

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:48.860
in a boy's food and drink to
make him fall in love with her.

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:51.540
This is exactly what
Maja does to Christian.

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:54.870
You can see here that his drink
is darker than the others,

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:56.600
with a reddish tint.

00:05:56.600 --> 00:05:58.840
And he pulls a pubic hair out of his mouth

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:00.940
after taking a bite of the pie.

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:04.005
While the most horrific events
in the film are fictitious,

00:06:04.005 --> 00:06:07.130
the holiday and many of
its rituals are very real.

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:10.440
Midsummer is an annual event
that takes place in mid-June,

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:12.150
around the summer solstice.

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:16.600
It's popular across the globe,
particularly in Scandinavia.

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:20.070
And it's even depicted briefly
in the Disney movie "Frozen."

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:22.947
Midsummer was initially a pagan holiday,

00:06:22.947 --> 00:06:25.470
but it eventually merged
with the Christian feast day

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:27.100
of St. John the Baptist,

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:29.110
a celebration of the prophet's birth.

00:06:29.110 --> 00:06:31.820
Indeed, there are several
references to John the Baptist

00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:32.760
in the film.

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:36.310
Pelle tells Dani that the
festival lasts nine days

00:06:36.310 --> 00:06:38.083
and that the event is special because

00:06:38.083 --> 00:06:40.480
it only occurs every 90 years.

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:42.529
In the Bible, the Virgin Mary stayed with

00:06:42.529 --> 00:06:44.420
John the Baptist's mother, Elizabeth,

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:47.140
for 90 days before Elizabeth gave birth.

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:50.215
Then, when they get to the
field outside the village,

00:06:50.215 --> 00:06:53.760
Ingemar wishes Pelle a
happy St. John's in Swedish.

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:55.770
One of the flowers that covers the village

00:06:55.770 --> 00:06:57.773
is St. John's wort.

00:06:57.773 --> 00:07:01.120
It's customary to celebrate
St. John's Day with bonfires,

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:03.390
like the one that burned
the sacrifices alive

00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:04.890
in the final scene,

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:07.848
and with torches, like those
that the villagers carry

00:07:07.848 --> 00:07:09.140
throughout the film.

00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:12.260
One of the key components of
"Midsommar" is the maypole,

00:07:12.260 --> 00:07:15.410
which we see in the film
covered in flowers and greenery

00:07:15.410 --> 00:07:16.540
for good luck.

00:07:16.540 --> 00:07:19.950
The ritual in which the villagers
dance around the maypole

00:07:19.950 --> 00:07:22.930
is based in a real-life Midsummer custom,

00:07:22.930 --> 00:07:26.670
one that revolves around
procreation and fertility.

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:29.730
So it makes sense that
Christian's bizarre sex ritual

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:32.360
is a major part of what comes next.

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:34.730
The community makes way for new life

00:07:34.730 --> 00:07:38.050
by making a lot of bloody sacrifices.

00:07:38.050 --> 00:07:40.270
Let's start with what happened to Josh.

00:07:40.270 --> 00:07:42.103
When he goes to bed on his last night,

00:07:42.103 --> 00:07:44.410
he's already set on sneaking into the barn

00:07:44.410 --> 00:07:46.320
to photograph the holy book.

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:48.197
The camera lingers on his shoes

00:07:48.197 --> 00:07:50.813
as he pulls the blanket over his body.

00:07:50.813 --> 00:07:52.910
The shot of his sneakers
may be referencing

00:07:52.910 --> 00:07:57.100
the mass suicide of the
Heaven's Gate cult in 1997,

00:07:57.100 --> 00:08:01.250
when 39 cult members were
found dead on their bunk beds,

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:03.340
having taken a powerful sedative

00:08:03.340 --> 00:08:06.270
and covered themselves in purple sheets.

00:08:06.270 --> 00:08:08.150
As an anthropology student,

00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:11.760
Josh is obsessed with northern
European pagan traditions,

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:15.590
to the extent where he's fully
embraced this cult in Sweden.

00:08:15.590 --> 00:08:17.740
His ambition and academic passion

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:20.490
is ultimately what brings his demise.

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:22.970
In the barn, Josh is attacked by Ulf,

00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:24.960
the same man who got angry at Mark

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:27.610
for peeing on the ancestral tree.

00:08:27.610 --> 00:08:32.120
The most disturbing part: Ulf
is now wearing Mark's face.

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:34.110
Later, we see the skin of Mark's face

00:08:34.110 --> 00:08:38.230
placed on a straw dummy and
topped off with a jester's hat.

00:08:38.230 --> 00:08:41.537
This cruel fate is foreshadowed
earlier in the movie.

00:08:41.537 --> 00:08:44.229
Simon: What are they playing?
Ingemar: Skin the Fool.

00:08:44.229 --> 00:08:46.583
[Narrator] In retrospect,
this bit of foreshadowing

00:08:46.583 --> 00:08:49.477
makes sense, as Mark pretty neatly fit

00:08:49.477 --> 00:08:51.760
the comedic archetype of the fool.

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:54.380
An even greater horror is done to Simon,

00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:56.860
the Londoner who mysteriously disappeared

00:08:56.860 --> 00:08:58.920
without his fiancée, Connie.

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:00.570
Christian stumbles on Simon

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:02.853
tied face-down in the chicken coop,

00:09:02.853 --> 00:09:06.802
with his back sliced open
and liver and ribs exposed.

00:09:06.802 --> 00:09:10.469
As we look closer, we see
that his lungs are moving,

00:09:10.469 --> 00:09:13.060
indicating that Simon is still alive.

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:14.500
If you're wondering how anyone

00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:16.214
could come up with this stuff,

00:09:16.214 --> 00:09:18.584
it turns out Aster did months of research

00:09:18.584 --> 00:09:20.510
on Viking torture techniques.

00:09:20.510 --> 00:09:22.930
What Simon's subjected to is right in line

00:09:22.930 --> 00:09:26.380
with the "blood eagle" method
of execution by torture,

00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:28.390
a prolonged type of ritual killing

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:30.380
detailed in Norse poetry.

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:33.040
So why have such brutal Viking rituals

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:35.490
survived in this insular community?

00:09:35.490 --> 00:09:37.020
And what does it all mean?

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:39.520
To get an idea, we should look to Ruben,

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:42.130
the so-called oracle in the community.

00:09:42.130 --> 00:09:44.690
As an elder in the village explains,

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:47.870
Ruben was purposely inbred
to serve as a conduit

00:09:47.870 --> 00:09:49.800
for the word of the gods.

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:52.620
The boy is said to have unclouded judgment

00:09:52.620 --> 00:09:55.750
as a result of his condition
and his pure blood.

00:09:55.750 --> 00:09:56.880
Towards the end of the movie,

00:09:56.880 --> 00:09:59.673
he's shown sitting on a
seat covered in cotton,

00:09:59.673 --> 00:10:02.485
looking almost like
he's sitting on a cloud.

00:10:02.485 --> 00:10:04.647
In Aster's words, Ruben embodies

00:10:04.647 --> 00:10:06.839
the political message of the film,

00:10:06.839 --> 00:10:09.790
which is perhaps partly
critiquing the global rise

00:10:09.790 --> 00:10:13.760
of xenophobia and the return
of the far right in Sweden.

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:15.647
As Aster said in an interview,

00:10:15.647 --> 00:10:17.643
"If you consider Swedish history,

00:10:17.643 --> 00:10:19.793
it is a very closed society.

00:10:19.793 --> 00:10:22.051
And what does that really mean?

00:10:22.051 --> 00:10:24.040
There are things happening
in Sweden right now

00:10:24.040 --> 00:10:25.730
that are echoes of things that happened

00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:27.650
in the Second World War."

00:10:27.650 --> 00:10:29.950
OK, now back to the ending of the film.

00:10:29.950 --> 00:10:32.110
It seems that the burning
of the sacred temple

00:10:32.110 --> 00:10:35.150
was part of Pelle's plan
from the very beginning.

00:10:35.150 --> 00:10:37.940
One theory: While Dani's family seemed

00:10:37.940 --> 00:10:39.970
to die in a murder-suicide,

00:10:39.970 --> 00:10:42.350
what if it was actually a setup?

00:10:42.350 --> 00:10:45.150
Next to her parents' bed
was a crown of flowers,

00:10:45.150 --> 00:10:46.930
and there are similar yellow flowers

00:10:46.930 --> 00:10:48.660
on their wallpaper too,

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:51.080
a mysterious bit of foreshadowing.

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:53.760
What if, say, Pelle killed Dani's family

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:55.570
to trigger the series of events

00:10:55.570 --> 00:10:57.360
that led her to the festival?

00:10:57.360 --> 00:10:59.800
He does emphasize that he
was most looking forward

00:10:59.800 --> 00:11:03.730
to Dani coming along on the
trip, more than anyone else.

00:11:03.730 --> 00:11:06.752
Whether or not Dani was chosen by Pelle,

00:11:06.752 --> 00:11:08.803
there are some other
hints that she was always

00:11:08.803 --> 00:11:11.330
a perfect candidate for May Queen.

00:11:11.330 --> 00:11:14.610
One is her birthday,
which Pelle is well aware

00:11:14.610 --> 00:11:17.270
coincides with the
beginning of the festival.

00:11:17.270 --> 00:11:20.890
Dani's in her mid-20s, which
is significant because,

00:11:20.890 --> 00:11:22.180
as Pelle explains,

00:11:22.180 --> 00:11:26.530
his community thinks of one's
life in terms of seasons.

00:11:26.530 --> 00:11:30.200
The first 16 years of life
are equivalent to one season,

00:11:30.200 --> 00:11:31.200
spring.

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:33.783
Dani is midway through
the next season, summer,

00:11:33.783 --> 00:11:35.970
so literally midsummer.

00:11:35.970 --> 00:11:38.600
Regardless of the impetus
for Dani's journey,

00:11:38.600 --> 00:11:41.840
the movie comes full
tragic circle by the end,

00:11:41.840 --> 00:11:44.241
beginning with the deaths of her family

00:11:44.241 --> 00:11:46.120
and ending with the death of Christian.

00:11:46.120 --> 00:11:49.578
As Aster says, "You start
with the unfathomable,

00:11:49.578 --> 00:11:51.982
and you end with the unfathomable."

